      "hints": [
        "Set $y = -\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4 - x^2}}$ and square both sides: $y^2 = \\frac{x^2}{4 - x^2}$.",
        "Rearrange to $x^2(1 + y^2) = 4y^2 \\implies x^2 = \\frac{4y^2}{1 + y^2}$.",
        "Select the negative square root to preserve opposite signs of $x$ and $y$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Set $y = -\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4 - x^2}}$ and square both sides:\n$$y^2 = \\frac{x^2}{4 - x^2} \\implies y^2(4 - x^2) = x^2$$",
        "Expand and isolate the $x^2$ terms:\n$$4y^2 - x^2 y^2 = x^2 \\implies x^2(1 + y^2) = 4y^2 \\implies x^2 = \\frac{4y^2}{1 + y^2}$$",
        "Take square roots, selecting the negative branch because $y$ and $x$ have opposite signs:\n$$x = -\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{1 + y^2}}$$",
        "Write the inverse function in terms of $x$:\n$$f^{-1}(x) = -\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{1 + x^2}}$$"
      ],
